IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ucta-nouns`
IMPORT `ucta-seat`

-- Worked cases for ucta-seat.l4: at least one per question, with the facts a person in the seat would state.
-- Every expected value is written from the text, beside the assertion; none is copied from the output.
-- JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act gives; NOTHING means the Act does not answer on these facts.


§ `Section 1 and the First Schedule - does the Act reach my case?`

-- s 1(1)(a): a breach of a contractual duty to take reasonable care is negligence.
#ASSERT `Is the breach negligence` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 1(1): negligence means (a) or (b); a stricter duty is neither, so it is not negligence.
#ASSERT `Is the breach negligence` FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 1(3): liability from things done in the course of a business.
#ASSERT `Is the liability business liability` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 1(3): any other liability is not business liability.
#ASSERT `Is the liability business liability` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 1(4): immaterial whether the breach was inadvertent or intentional, direct or vicarious.
#ASSERT `Does inadvertent or intentional make a difference` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 1(4) speaks only of those two distinctions.
#ASSERT `Does inadvertent or intentional make a difference` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Business liability, a sale of goods, none of the Schedule matters, Part 3 no: no exception takes section 3 out of the case.
#ASSERT `Do the scope rules take my case out of the section` `Section 3` TRUE `A contract of sale of goods` `None of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE No FALSE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- First Schedule para 1(a): sections 2 to 4 do not extend to any contract of insurance.
#ASSERT `Do the scope rules take my case out of the section` `Section 3` TRUE `Another kind of contract` `Insurance, including a contract to pay an annuity on human life` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE No FALSE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Para 2: sections 2 to 4 do not extend to marine salvage except in favour of a consumer; consumer status unknown, so it cannot be told.
#ASSERT `Do the scope rules take my case out of the section` `Section 2(2)` TRUE `Another kind of contract` `Marine salvage or towage` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `I am not sure` FALSE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 1(3) limits sections 2 to 7 to business liability, but s 6(4) extends section 6 to any contract of sale of goods: no exception.
#ASSERT `Do the scope rules take my case out of the section` `Section 6` FALSE `A contract of sale of goods` `None of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE No FALSE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Section 7 is not within s 6(4): a non-business liability is out.
#ASSERT `Do the scope rules take my case out of the section` `Section 7` FALSE `A contract of sale of goods` `None of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE No FALSE No EQUALS JUST TRUE

§ `Section 2 - negligence liability`

-- s 2(1): a person cannot exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence.
#ASSERT `Can the other party exclude liability for death or injury from negligence` TRUE TRUE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Other loss or damage is subsection (2).
#ASSERT `Can the other party exclude liability for death or injury from negligence` FALSE TRUE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- An exception takes section 2(1) out of the case.
#ASSERT `Can the other party exclude liability for death or injury from negligence` TRUE TRUE Yes EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(2): for other loss, the bar lifts so far as the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.
#ASSERT `Must the clause for other loss satisfy reasonableness under section 2` FALSE TRUE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Death or personal injury is subsection (1), which has no such exception.
#ASSERT `Must the clause for other loss satisfy reasonableness under section 2` TRUE TRUE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- A flat negative takes section 2(2) out of the case (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 'do not extend to' the contract): no requirement of reasonableness applies under it.
#ASSERT `Must the clause for other loss satisfy reasonableness under section 2` FALSE TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Whether the scope rules take the case out cannot be told.
#ASSERT `Must the clause for other loss satisfy reasonableness under section 2` FALSE TRUE `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(2): a person cannot exclude liability for negligence except so far as the term is reasonable; it is not.
#ASSERT `Can the other party exclude liability for other loss from negligence` FALSE TRUE No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The term is reasonable: the bar is lifted and the subsection grants nothing (ruling QA).
#ASSERT `Can the other party exclude liability for other loss from negligence` FALSE TRUE Yes No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Nobody has shown that the term is reasonable: it is for the party claiming that it is to show it (s 11(5)), so the bar stands.
#ASSERT `Can the other party exclude liability for other loss from negligence` FALSE TRUE `I am not sure` No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 2(3): agreement or awareness is not of itself voluntary acceptance of any risk.
#ASSERT `Does my agreement to the clause mean I accepted the risk` TRUE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Subsection (3) speaks of a term or notice that purports to exclude liability for negligence.
#ASSERT `Does my agreement to the clause mean I accepted the risk` FALSE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(3): where a term or notice purports to exclude liability for negligence, agreement or awareness is not of itself acceptance of the risk.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(3) stop my agreement counting as accepting the risk` TRUE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The 'Where' is not met: nothing purports to exclude liability for negligence.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(3) stop my agreement counting as accepting the risk` FALSE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A flat negative takes the section out of the case.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(3) stop my agreement counting as accepting the risk` TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Section 3 - liability arising in contract`

-- s 3(1): contracting parties, one of whom deals as consumer.
#ASSERT `Does section 3 apply to my contract` TRUE Yes FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 3(1): or deals on the other's written standard terms of business.
#ASSERT `Does section 3 apply to my contract` TRUE No TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Neither limb: the 'where' is not met.
#ASSERT `Does section 3 apply to my contract` TRUE No FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Consumer status unknown and no standard terms.
#ASSERT `Does section 3 apply to my contract` TRUE `I am not sure` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 3(2)(b)(i): a term claiming substantially different performance, except so far as reasonable.
#ASSERT `Must the clause satisfy reasonableness under section 3` Yes `A contract term` `Claims a right to render a contractual performance substantially different from that reasonably expected` FALSE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Section 3 does not apply between these parties.
#ASSERT `Must the clause satisfy reasonableness under section 3` No `A contract term` `Claims a right to render a contractual performance substantially different from that reasonably expected` FALSE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Section 3(2) speaks of a contract term.
#ASSERT `Must the clause satisfy reasonableness under section 3` Yes `A notice that does not have contractual effect` `Claims a right to render a contractual performance substantially different from that reasonably expected` FALSE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- First Schedule paragraph 1(a): sections 2 to 4 do not extend to a contract of insurance; no requirement of reasonableness applies under section 3.
#ASSERT `Must the clause satisfy reasonableness under section 3` Yes `A contract term` `Claims a right to render no performance at all, in respect of the whole or part of the obligation` FALSE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Whether the scope rules take the case out cannot be told.
#ASSERT `Must the clause satisfy reasonableness under section 3` Yes `A contract term` `Claims a right to render no performance at all, in respect of the whole or part of the obligation` FALSE `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 3(2)(b)(ii): a term claiming to render no performance at all cannot be relied on except so far as reasonable; it is not.
#ASSERT `Can the other party rely on the clause under section 3` Yes `A contract term` `Claims a right to render no performance at all, in respect of the whole or part of the obligation` FALSE No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The term is reasonable: the bar is lifted and the subsection grants nothing (ruling QA).
#ASSERT `Can the other party rely on the clause under section 3` Yes `A contract term` `Excludes or restricts the other party's liability for its own breach of contract` TRUE Yes No EQUALS NOTHING
-- (a) speaks of a party 'when himself in breach of contract'; the other party is not.
#ASSERT `Can the other party rely on the clause under section 3` Yes `A contract term` `Excludes or restricts the other party's liability for its own breach of contract` FALSE No No EQUALS NOTHING
-- The section is out of the case: the Act then says nothing about whether the other party can rely on the term.
#ASSERT `Can the other party rely on the clause under section 3` Yes `A contract term` `Claims a right to render no performance at all, in respect of the whole or part of the obligation` FALSE No Yes EQUALS NOTHING
-- Nobody has shown that the term is reasonable (s 11(5)): the bar of section 3(2) stands.
#ASSERT `Can the other party rely on the clause under section 3` Yes `A contract term` `Claims a right to render no performance at all, in respect of the whole or part of the obligation` FALSE `I am not sure` No EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Section 4 - unreasonable indemnity clauses`

-- s 4(1): a person dealing as consumer cannot be made to indemnify, except so far as the term is reasonable.
#ASSERT `Must the indemnity clause satisfy reasonableness` Yes TRUE `A contract term` No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Section 4(1) speaks of a person dealing as consumer.
#ASSERT `Must the indemnity clause satisfy reasonableness` No TRUE `A contract term` No EQUALS NOTHING
-- A flat negative takes section 4 out of the case: no requirement of reasonableness applies under it.
#ASSERT `Must the indemnity clause satisfy reasonableness` Yes TRUE `A contract term` Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 4(1): the term is not reasonable, so the consumer cannot be made to indemnify.
#ASSERT `Can I be made to indemnify another person` Yes TRUE `A contract term` No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The term is reasonable: the bar is lifted and the subsection grants nothing (ruling QA).
#ASSERT `Can I be made to indemnify another person` Yes TRUE `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Nobody has shown that the term is reasonable (s 11(5)): the bar of section 4(1) stands.
#ASSERT `Can I be made to indemnify another person` Yes TRUE `A contract term` `I am not sure` No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 4(2)(a): applies whether the liability is the person's directly or incurred vicariously.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 apply to this liability` `Directly by the person to be indemnified` `To the person dealing as consumer` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- (a) names direct and vicarious liability only.
#ASSERT `Does section 4 apply to this liability` `In another way` `To someone else` EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 5 - guarantee of consumer goods`

-- s 5(2)(a): used or held for use otherwise than exclusively for a business.
#ASSERT `Are the goods in consumer use` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 5(2)(a): used exclusively for the purposes of a business, so not in consumer use.
#ASSERT `Are the goods in consumer use` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 5(2)(b): anything in writing that contains a promise or assurance that defects will be made good.
#ASSERT `Is the document a guarantee` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 5(2)(b): no promise or assurance of making defects good.
#ASSERT `Is the document a guarantee` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 5(3): the section does not apply between the parties to a contract under which possession or ownership of the goods passed.
#ASSERT `Does section 5 apply between us` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Subsection (3) disapplies the section only between those parties.
#ASSERT `Does section 5 apply between us` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 5(1): consumer goods proving defective through the negligence of a manufacturer or distributor; the clause is in a guarantee; liability cannot be excluded.
#ASSERT `Can liability be excluded by a guarantee` Yes TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 5(3): the section does not apply between the parties to the contract under which the goods passed.
#ASSERT `Can liability be excluded by a guarantee` Yes TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- The goods are not of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption.
#ASSERT `Can liability be excluded by a guarantee` No TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 5(1): consumer goods proving defective through a manufacturer's or distributor's negligence; the clause is in a guarantee: liability cannot be excluded.
#ASSERT `Does section 5 stop liability being excluded by a guarantee` Yes TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The 'where' is not met: the loss does not arise from the goods proving defective.
#ASSERT `Does section 5 stop liability being excluded by a guarantee` Yes TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 5(3): the section does not apply between the parties to the contract under which the goods passed.
#ASSERT `Does section 5 stop liability being excluded by a guarantee` Yes TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The goods are not of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption: a scope limit with no 'only'.
#ASSERT `Does section 5 stop liability being excluded by a guarantee` No TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- A flat negative takes the section out of the case.
#ASSERT `Does section 5 stop liability being excluded by a guarantee` Yes TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Section 6 - sale and hire-purchase`

-- s 6(4): includes liabilities under any contract of sale of goods.
#ASSERT `Is the liability one that section 6 covers` FALSE `A contract of sale of goods` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 6(4) 'include': the Act names business liabilities and sale and hire-purchase.
#ASSERT `Is the liability one that section 6 covers` FALSE `Another kind of contract` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 6(1): liability under section 12 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 cannot be excluded by any contract term.
#ASSERT `Can the seller exclude liability for title` `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 12` `A contract term` No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Subsection (1) names only the title obligations.
#ASSERT `Can the seller exclude liability for title` `Another obligation` `A contract term` No EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 6(2): as against a consumer the quality liability cannot be excluded, however reasonable the term.
#ASSERT `Can the seller exclude liability for quality` Yes `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 6(3): against a non-consumer it can be excluded, so far as the term is reasonable; it is.
#ASSERT `Can the seller exclude liability for quality` No `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 6(3): only so far as reasonable; it is not.
#ASSERT `Can the seller exclude liability for quality` No `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Consumer status unknown, term unreasonable: subsections (2) and (3) both say no.
#ASSERT `Can the seller exclude liability for quality` `I am not sure` `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Consumer status unknown, term reasonable: (2) says no and (3) says yes.
#ASSERT `Can the seller exclude liability for quality` `I am not sure` `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Against a non-consumer the term is effective only so far as it is reasonable (s 6(3)); nobody has shown that it is (s 11(5)).
#ASSERT `Can the seller exclude liability for quality` No `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` `I am not sure` No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 6(3): against a person dealing otherwise than as consumer, the term is effective only so far as reasonable.
#ASSERT `Must the quality term satisfy reasonableness under section 6` No `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Against a consumer subsection (2) governs, with no reasonableness exception.
#ASSERT `Must the quality term satisfy reasonableness under section 6` Yes `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` No EQUALS NOTHING
-- A flat negative takes section 6 out of the case: no requirement of reasonableness applies under it.
#ASSERT `Must the quality term satisfy reasonableness under section 6` No `Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 13, 14 or 15` `A contract term` Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Section 7 - miscellaneous contracts under which goods pass`

-- s 7(1): goods pass under a contract not governed by the law of sale of goods or hire-purchase.
#ASSERT `Do the rules in section 7 apply to my contract` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 7(1): a sale of goods is governed by the law of sale of goods.
#ASSERT `Do the rules in section 7 apply to my contract` `A contract of sale of goods` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 7(2): against a consumer, liability for quality or fitness cannot be excluded.
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for description or quality` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` Yes `Quality or fitness of the goods for a particular purpose` `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 7(3): against a non-consumer it can, so far as the term is reasonable; it is.
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for description or quality` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` No `Quality or fitness of the goods for a particular purpose` `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Section 7 applies only where goods pass under a contract not governed by the law of sale of goods or hire-purchase.
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for description or quality` `A contract of sale of goods` No `Quality or fitness of the goods for a particular purpose` `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Against a non-consumer the term is effective only so far as it is reasonable (s 7(3)); nobody has shown that it is (s 11(5)).
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for description or quality` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` No `Quality or fitness of the goods for a particular purpose` `A contract term` `I am not sure` No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 7(3): the term is effective only so far as it satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.
#ASSERT `Must the term satisfy reasonableness under section 7(3)` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` No `Correspondence of the goods with description or sample` `A contract term` No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Against a consumer subsection (2) governs.
#ASSERT `Must the term satisfy reasonableness under section 7(3)` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` Yes `Correspondence of the goods with description or sample` `A contract term` No EQUALS NOTHING
-- A flat negative takes section 7 out of the case: no requirement of reasonableness applies under it.
#ASSERT `Must the term satisfy reasonableness under section 7(3)` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` No `Correspondence of the goods with description or sample` `A contract term` Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 7(3A): liability under section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 cannot be excluded by such a term.
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for title under the 1982 Act` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` `Supply of Goods Act 1982 section 2 (implied terms about title)` `A contract term` No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Subsection (3A) names section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 only.
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for title under the 1982 Act` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` `Quality or fitness of the goods for a particular purpose` `A contract term` No EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 7(4): in a case to which (3A) does not apply, the bar lifts so far as the term is reasonable.
#ASSERT `Must the term for ownership or quiet possession satisfy reasonableness` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` `The right to transfer ownership of the goods, or give possession` FALSE `A contract term` No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Subsection (3A) applies: it governs and has no reasonableness exception.
#ASSERT `Must the term for ownership or quiet possession satisfy reasonableness` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` `The right to transfer ownership of the goods, or give possession` TRUE `A contract term` No EQUALS NOTHING
-- A flat negative takes section 7 out of the case: no requirement of reasonableness applies under it.
#ASSERT `Must the term for ownership or quiet possession satisfy reasonableness` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` `The right to transfer ownership of the goods, or give possession` FALSE `A contract term` Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 7(4): the term is not reasonable, so liability cannot be excluded.
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for ownership or quiet possession` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` `The right to transfer ownership of the goods, or give possession` FALSE `A contract term` No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The term is reasonable: the bar is lifted and the subsection grants nothing (ruling QA).
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for ownership or quiet possession` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` `The right to transfer ownership of the goods, or give possession` FALSE `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS NOTHING
-- Nobody has shown that the term is reasonable (s 11(5)): the bar of section 7(4) stands.
#ASSERT `Can the supplier exclude liability for ownership or quiet possession` `A contract, not of sale of goods or hire-purchase, under which possession or ownership of goods passes` `The right to transfer ownership of the goods, or give possession` FALSE `A contract term` `I am not sure` No EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Sections 9 and 10 - effect of breach; secondary contracts`

-- s 9(1): a term that has to be reasonable may be found so notwithstanding termination.
#ASSERT `Can the clause be given effect after termination` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The contract has not been terminated: subsection (1) says nothing.
#ASSERT `Can the clause be given effect after termination` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 9(2): affirming does not of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness.
#ASSERT `Does affirming the contract exclude reasonableness` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- No affirmation: subsection (2) says nothing.
#ASSERT `Does affirming the contract exclude reasonableness` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 9(2): where on a breach the contract is nevertheless affirmed, this does not of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness.
#ASSERT `Does affirming the contract leave the requirement of reasonableness in place` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The 'Where' is not met: the contract was not affirmed.
#ASSERT `Does affirming the contract leave the requirement of reasonableness in place` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 10: a person is not bound by a term taking away rights to enforce a liability this Part prevents being excluded.
#ASSERT `Am I bound by the secondary contract term` TRUE TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- This Part does not prevent the exclusion: the section says nothing.
#ASSERT `Am I bound by the secondary contract term` TRUE TRUE No EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Sections 11 to 14 - reasonableness, dealing as consumer, varieties of exemption clause, definitions`

-- s 11(1): the court's judgment is that it is reasonable.
#ASSERT `Does the clause satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` `A contract term` Yes No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 11(1): the court's judgment is that it is not.
#ASSERT `Does the clause satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` `A contract term` No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 29(2): a term approved by a competent authority is taken as reasonable.
#ASSERT `Does the clause satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` `A contract term` No Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The court's judgment is not known and s 29(2) does not apply.  This question asks what the court found, not who must show it (s 11(5)); the questions on whether the other party can exclude or rely treat 'not sure' as not shown.
#ASSERT `Does the clause satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` `A contract term` `I am not sure` No EQUALS NOTHING
-- A notice having contractual effect is neither a contract term (s 11(1)) nor a notice not having contractual effect (s 11(3)).
#ASSERT `Does the clause satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` `A notice that has contractual effect` Yes No EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 11(2) and the Second Schedule (a) to (e).
#ASSERT `Which matters must the court consider for sections 6 and 7` TRUE EQUALS JUST (LIST `Bargaining positions and alternative means of meeting the customer's requirements`, `Inducement, or an opportunity to contract elsewhere without a similar term`, `Whether the customer knew or ought reasonably to have known of the term`, `Whether compliance with a condition was practicable`, `Whether the goods were made, processed or adapted to the customer's special order`)
-- Subsection (2) speaks of sections 6 and 7 only.
#ASSERT `Which matters must the court consider for sections 6 and 7` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 11(2): this subsection does not prevent the court holding, in accordance with a rule of law, that the term is not a term of the contract.
#ASSERT `Does section 11 stop the court finding the term is not part of the contract` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The text speaks of a holding in accordance with a rule of law.
#ASSERT `Does section 11 stop the court finding the term is not part of the contract` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 11(4)(a) and (b).
#ASSERT `Which matters must the court consider for a capped liability` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST (LIST `The resources the party could expect to be available to meet the liability`, `How far it was open to the party to cover itself by insurance`)
-- The 'Where' of s 11(4) is not met.
#ASSERT `Which matters must the court consider for a capped liability` FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 11(5): it is for those claiming reasonableness to show it.
#ASSERT `Must the other party show the clause is reasonable` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Subsection (5) places the burden on those claiming reasonableness only.
#ASSERT `Must the other party show the clause is reasonable` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 12(1): not in business, the other party in business, goods of a private type.
#ASSERT `Do I deal as consumer` FALSE FALSE TRUE `A contract of sale of goods` Yes FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 12(1)(a): the party made the contract in the course of a business.
#ASSERT `Do I deal as consumer` TRUE FALSE TRUE `A contract of sale of goods` Yes FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 12(2): on a sale by auction or competitive tender the buyer is never a consumer.
#ASSERT `Do I deal as consumer` FALSE FALSE TRUE `A contract of sale of goods` Yes TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Whether the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use is not known.
#ASSERT `Do I deal as consumer` FALSE FALSE TRUE `A contract of sale of goods` `I am not sure` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Limb (c) applies to sale, hire-purchase and section 7 contracts only.
#ASSERT `Do I deal as consumer` FALSE FALSE TRUE `Another kind of contract` `I am not sure` FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 12(3): it is for those claiming that a party does not deal as consumer to show that he does not.
#ASSERT `Who must show I do not deal as consumer` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Subsection (3) does not say what the party claiming to be a consumer must show.
#ASSERT `Who must show I do not deal as consumer` FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 13(1)(a): to the extent Part 1 prevents the exclusion, it also prevents restrictive or onerous conditions.
#ASSERT `Can the other party avoid the liability by another kind of clause` `Makes the liability or its enforcement subject to restrictive or onerous conditions` Yes `Section 3` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The last limb of s 13(1) names sections 2 and 5 to 7, not section 4.
#ASSERT `Can the other party avoid the liability by another kind of clause` `Excludes or restricts the relevant obligation or duty itself` Yes `Section 4` EQUALS NOTHING
-- 'To the extent that this Part prevents': it does not.
#ASSERT `Can the other party avoid the liability by another kind of clause` `Excludes or restricts rules of evidence or procedure` No `Section 3` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 13(2): an agreement in writing to arbitrate is not treated as excluding or restricting liability.
#ASSERT `Is an arbitration agreement an exemption clause` `An agreement in writing to submit present or future differences to arbitration` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The text speaks of an agreement in writing.
#ASSERT `Is an arbitration agreement an exemption clause` `An agreement in writing to submit present or future differences to arbitration` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 14: business includes a profession.
#ASSERT `Is this activity a business` `A profession` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 'includes': the list is not closed.
#ASSERT `Is this activity a business` `Another activity` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 14: personal injury includes any disease.
#ASSERT `Is this harm personal injury` `A disease` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 'includes': a bodily injury is not named.
#ASSERT `Is this harm personal injury` `A bodily injury` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 14: notice includes an announcement.
#ASSERT `Is this communication a notice` `An announcement, in writing` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 'includes': the list is not closed.
#ASSERT `Is this communication a notice` `Something else` EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Part 3 - international supply contracts, choice of law, sea carriage, other legislation, transitional`

-- s 26(3)(a), (b) and (4)(a): a sale, parties in different States, goods carried from one State to another.
#ASSERT `Is my contract an international supply contract` `A contract of sale of goods` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 26(4): none of (a), (b) or (c) is met.
#ASSERT `Is my contract an international supply contract` `A contract of sale of goods` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 26(1): the limits do not apply to liability under an international supply contract.
#ASSERT `Do the Act's limits apply to my international supply contract` `A contract of sale of goods` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Subsection (1) speaks only of such a contract.
#ASSERT `Do the Act's limits apply to my international supply contract` `A contract of sale of goods` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 26(2): not subject to any requirement of reasonableness under section 3 or 4.
#ASSERT `Must the terms of my international supply contract be reasonable` `A contract of sale of goods` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 27(1): where Singapore law is the proper law only by choice of the parties, sections 2 to 7 do not operate as part of the proper law.
#ASSERT `Do sections 2 to 7 apply when Singapore law is only chosen` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Subsection (1) is conditioned by 'Where'.
#ASSERT `Do sections 2 to 7 apply when Singapore law is only chosen` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 27(1): where Singapore law is the proper law only by choice of the parties, sections 2 to 7 do not operate as part of the proper law.
#ASSERT `Do sections 2 to 7 stop operating when Singapore law is only chosen` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The 'Where' is not met: Singapore law is the proper law for another reason, so the subsection does not switch the sections off.
#ASSERT `Do sections 2 to 7 stop operating when Singapore law is only chosen` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 27(2)(a): the term appears to have been imposed to evade the Act.
#ASSERT `Does the Act protect me despite a foreign law clause` TRUE Yes No FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 27(2)(b): a consumer habitually resident in Singapore, with the essential steps taken there.
#ASSERT `Does the Act protect me despite a foreign law clause` TRUE No Yes TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Neither limb: the 'where' is not met.
#ASSERT `Does the Act protect me despite a foreign law clause` TRUE No No FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- There is no foreign-law term: subsection (2) says nothing.
#ASSERT `Does the Act protect me despite a foreign law clause` FALSE No No FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 28(1): a contract for carriage by sea of a passenger.
#ASSERT `Does section 28 apply to my sea carriage contract` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Not within subsection (1).
#ASSERT `Does section 28 apply to my sea carriage contract` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 28(2): not precluded to the extent the Minister's order prescribes.
#ASSERT `Does the Act stop the sea carrier excluding liability` TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The order does not prescribe it.
#ASSERT `Does the Act stop the sea carrier excluding liability` TRUE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 29(1)(a): authorised or required by an enactment.
#ASSERT `Does the Act override a provision required by legislation or treaty` TRUE `Legislation of Singapore` FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 29(1)(b): made to comply with an international agreement and not more restrictive than it contemplates.
#ASSERT `Does the Act override a provision required by legislation or treaty` FALSE `Something else` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Neither limb: subsection (1) says nothing.
#ASSERT `Does the Act override a provision required by legislation or treaty` FALSE `Something else` FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 29(2): approved by a competent authority acting under a statutory function, not itself a party.
#ASSERT `Is a term approved by an authority taken as reasonable` TRUE `A court` `Legislation of Singapore` FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 29(2): the competent authority is itself a party to the contract.
#ASSERT `Is a term approved by an authority taken as reasonable` TRUE `A court` `Legislation of Singapore` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 30(2): nothing in the Act applies to a contract made before 12 November 1993, except so far as section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 applied it.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to a contract or loss before 12 November 1993` (Date 1 6 1992) (Date 1 3 2024) No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Both dates are after the day: subsection (2) says nothing.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to a contract or loss before 12 November 1993` (Date 1 6 1995) (Date 1 3 2024) No EQUALS NOTHING
-- The Civil Law Act exception is met (ruling QA).
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to a contract or loss before 12 November 1993` (Date 1 6 1992) (Date 1 3 2024) Yes EQUALS NOTHING
-- An international supply contract (s 26): Part 3 takes the case out.
#ASSERT `Does Part 3 take my case out of sections 2 to 7` `A contract of sale of goods` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE No FALSE `Something else` FALSE FALSE (Date 1 3 2024) (Date 1 3 2024) No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- No Part 3 provision applies.
#ASSERT `Does Part 3 take my case out of sections 2 to 7` `A contract of sale of goods` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE No FALSE `Something else` FALSE FALSE (Date 1 3 2024) (Date 1 3 2024) No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Sea carriage of a passenger and the Minister's order not known: cannot be told.
#ASSERT `Does Part 3 take my case out of sections 2 to 7` `A contract of sale of goods` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `I am not sure` FALSE `Something else` FALSE FALSE (Date 1 3 2024) (Date 1 3 2024) No EQUALS NOTHING
